dict(pas="11.21", w="子路=zǐ lù/Zilu — the disciple Zhong You | 問：「/asked | 聞/hearing (something) | 斯/then, straightway | 行=xíng/to carry (it) out | 諸？」=zhū/(= 之乎: 'it?') | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^有/there are | 父兄=fù xiōng/father and elder brothers//father//elder brother | 在，/living | ^如之何=rú zhī hé/how (could you)//like//it//what | 其/(softens the verb: then) | 聞/hearing | 斯/straightway | 行=xíng/carry out | 之？」/it | ^冉有=rǎn yǒu/Ran You — the disciple Ran Qiu | 問：「/asked | 聞/hearing (something) | 斯/straightway | 行=xíng/to carry (it) out | 諸？」=zhū/(= 之乎: 'it?') | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 聞/hearing | 斯/straightway | 行=xíng/carry out | 之。」/it | ^公西華=gōng xī huá/Gongxi Hua — the disciple Gongxi Chi | 曰：「/said | ^由/Yóu | 也/(pause: 'as for') | 問『/asked | 聞/hearing | 斯/straightway | 行=xíng/to carry out | 諸』，=zhū/it? | ^子/you, Master | 曰『/said | 有/there are | 父兄=fù xiōng/father and elder brothers | 在』；/living | ^求/Qiu | 也/(pause: 'as for') | 問『/asked | 聞/hearing | 斯/straightway | 行=xíng/to carry out | 諸』，=zhū/it? | ^子/you, Master | 曰『/said | 聞/hearing | 斯/straightway | 行=xíng/carry out | 之』。/it | ^赤/Chi — Gongxi Hua naming himself | 也/(pause) | 惑，/is perplexed | ^敢/(I) venture | 問。」/to ask | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^求/Qiu | 也/(pause: 'as for') | 退，/draws back | ^故/therefore | 進/(I) moved forward | 之；/him | ^由/Yóu | 也/(pause: 'as for') | 兼=jiān/doubles, does the part of two | 人，/men — outdoes others | ^故/therefore | 退/(I) drew back | 之。」/him",
  l="Zilu asked: “Hearing something, should one straightway do it?”\nThe Master said:\n“There are father and elder brothers living —\nhow could you, hearing, straightway do it?”\nRan You asked: “Hearing something, should one straightway do it?”\nThe Master said: “Hearing it, straightway do it.”\nGongxi Hua said:\n“Yóu asked, ‘Hearing something, should one straightway do it?’\nand you said, ‘There are father and elder brothers living’;\nQiu asked, ‘Hearing something, should one straightway do it?’\nand you said, ‘Hearing it, straightway do it.’\nChi is perplexed,\nand ventures to ask.”\nThe Master said:\n“Qiu draws back,\nso I moved him forward;\nYóu does the part of two men,\nso I drew him back.”",
  i="Zilu asked whether he should put a thing into practice the moment he heard it. The Master said: “Your father and elder brothers are still alive — how can you go ahead as soon as you hear?” Ran You asked the same question. The Master said: “Yes: what you hear, carry out at once.” Gongxi Hua said: “When Yóu asked, you reminded him that his father and elder brothers were alive. When Qiu asked, you told him to carry it out at once. I am puzzled, and venture to ask why.” The Master said: “Qiu is timid, so I gave him a push. Yóu is out to outdo everyone, so I put a check on him.”",
  n="Three disciples, each under two names: 子路 Zilu is Zhong You 仲由, whom the Master calls 由 Yóu; 冉有 Ran You is Ran Qiu 冉求, called 求 Qiu; 公西華 Gongxi Hua is Gongxi Chi 公西赤, who names himself 赤 Chi. The narrative uses the courtesy name, speech the personal name. 聞斯行諸 is a whole question in four characters: 斯 'then, straightway' ties the doing to the hearing, and the final 諸 is the fused 之乎 'it?'. 如之何其… 'how could…?', with 其 softening the verb. 進 'to advance' and 退 'to draw back' are used causatively: 進之 'I moved him forward', 退之 'I held him back'. 兼人 is literally 'to double a man'; Zhu Xi: 兼人謂勝人也, 'to outdo others'. Zhu Xi quotes Zhang Jingfu (Zhang Shi): on hearing what is right one should certainly be bold to do it, but while father and elder brothers live there are things one may not decide alone, and to act without taking their orders would itself harm what is right; Zilu, who feared to hear more before he had acted on what he had heard (5.13), was in no danger of failing to act — the danger was that he would overdo it and skip what needs their word; Ran Qiu's endowment erred toward weakness, and the danger was that he would hesitate and shrink from what he ought to do; the sage pushed one forward and held the other back to bring both within what is right, with neither excess nor shortfall. He Yan's Bao Xian takes the thing heard to be relief of the destitute; Kong Anguo: one should tell father and elder brothers and may not decide alone; Zheng Xuan: Ran You was modest and retiring by nature, Zilu bent on outdoing others, and each was corrected where he went wrong. Variant: He Yan's text ends the Master's first answer 如之何其聞斯行之也, with a final particle."),
# ---------------- 11.22 ----------------
dict(pas="11.22", w="子/the Master | 畏=wèi/was in danger, on his guard | 於/at | 匡，=kuāng/Kuang | ^顔淵=yán yuān/Yan Yuan | 後。/fell behind | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 吾/I | 以/took | 女=rǔ/you | 為=wéi/to be | 死/dead | 矣。」/(so it was) | ^曰：「/(he) said | 子/(while) you, Master | 在，/live | ^回/Hui | 何/how | 敢/would dare | 死？」/to die",
  l="The Master was in danger at Kuang, and Yan Yuan fell behind.\nThe Master said: “I took you for dead.”\nHe said: “While you, Master, live,\nhow would Hui dare to die?”",
  i="When the Master was in danger at Kuang, Yan Yuan was separated from him and caught up only later. The Master said: “I thought you had been killed.” He said: “As long as you are alive, Master, how could I let myself die?”",
  n="畏於匡 as at 9.5: 畏 wèi is here 'to be in danger, on one's guard', and Kuang the place where the people took the Master for Yang Hu and surrounded him. 後 'came behind': Zhu Xi, they lost each other and he was left behind; He Yan's Kong Anguo says the same. 女 is rǔ 'you' (Zhu Xi: 女音汝). 以 A 為 B 'to take A as B': 以女為死 'took you as dead'. In the answer 子 is 'you, Master', and Yan Yuan calls himself by his personal name, 回 Hui. 何敢死: Zhu Xi, he would not rush into the fight and get himself killed. He quotes Hu Yin at length: under the former kings' order a man lives by three — father, teacher and lord — serves them as one, and dies wherever his duty lies; between Yan Yuan and the Master both kindness and duty were complete, more than between ordinary teacher and pupil; had the Master met disaster, Hui would have thrown away his life to go to him, and had he himself survived he would have appealed to the Son of Heaven above and the regional lord below for a punitive expedition to avenge him; but with the Master alive, why would he not hold his life dear rather than run on the blades of the men of Kuang? He Yan's Bao Xian: with the Master alive, there was nothing for which he might dare to die."),
